    {
      "id": "n1fwKpyYkToCH1CGe7bvf",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -427.8306924642749,
      "y": 2286.091284839271,
      "width": 802,
      "height": 350,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b12",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1725756728,
      "version": 737,
      "versionNonce": 550408760,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1783745918123,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "5. Whenever we call add() method on LinkedList we a new Node Object is created \nwhich holds three things data, prev , next.\n\n6. Unlike ArrayList , LinkedList does not have any default capacity , whenever we\ncall add() only then java creates a new Node and attach it to existing node.\n\n7. Technically LinkedList does not have concept of indexing, whenever we\ncall get() java start traversing the LinkedList from the start.\n\n8. Bottom Line is that \n\nArrayList is good when application require frequent get() /accessing element.\n\nLinkedList is good when application require frequent addition and deletion.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "5. Whenever we call add() method on LinkedList we a new Node Object is created \nwhich holds three things data, prev , next.\n\n6. Unlike ArrayList , LinkedList does not have any default capacity , whenever we\ncall add() only then java creates a new Node and attach it to existing node.\n\n7. Technically LinkedList does not have concept of indexing, whenever we\ncall get() java start traversing the LinkedList from the start.\n\n8. Bottom Line is that \n\nArrayList is good when application require frequent get() /accessing element.\n\nLinkedList is good when application require frequent addition and deletion.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "lr9D_wjmgFH8Xv6gVicNj",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -428.57670805177395,
      "y": 2709.6754028885725,
      "width": 767.761357765631,
      "height": 50,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
